<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Pure voxel logic: storage, generation, meshing. No graphics API here so
       the whole simulation stays runnable from a headless CLI for audits. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Driftwood.Core</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ⛳ THE FIRST PAINTED ART THIS PROJECT SHIPS, and it is deliberately narrow.

       Every tile in the game is drawn in code by TileGen, which is what makes the whole set
       unambiguously ours and what lets a texture pack layer over a complete world rather than a
       half-finished one. That argument is about the WORLD. It has never been an argument about
       interface chrome, and a book on a button is exactly the case where sixteen procedural pixels
       lose to a drawing: it is one picture, seen at one size, that has to read as a book at a
       glance and can never be generated into being more legible.

       ⚠ Embedded rather than shipped beside the exe, like the window icons and unlike the sounds:
       it is a quarter of a megabyte, it is needed on the first frame an interface opens, and a
       missing file here is a button with a hole in it rather than a missing sound effect.

       ⚠ A pack can still replace it — it is a texture layer like any other, with a row in
       BlockTextureSet.Layers pointing at the file a pack would keep its own in. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\assets\recipe_book.png" LogicalName="Driftwood.Core.recipe-book.png" />

    <!-- ⛳ The health bar's heart, the user's own drawing, for the same reason the book is here: it
         is one small shape seen at one size whose whole job is to be recognised instantly, and a
         procedural heart is a blob that can never be argued into being a better heart.

         ⚠ It is an OUTLINE and the bar needs something that fills, so the inside is derived from it
         rather than drawn — see PaintedArt.HeartTile. The sheet holds seven copies of one heart;
         only the first is read, and it is FOUND rather than measured into a constant. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\assets\health.png" LogicalName="Driftwood.Core.health.png" />

    <!-- ⛳ And the hunger bar, which the user drew as a PAIR: foodbar.png is a finished full-colour
         drumstick and emptyfood.png is the same shape as a hollow outline. So this bar needs no
         derivation at all — the two states are both drawn, where the heart had to have its middle
         flooded because only the socket was. Measured: 30,123 distinct colours against 1,497. -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\assets\foodbar.png" LogicalName="Driftwood.Core.foodbar.png" />
    <EmbeddedResource Include="..\..\assets\emptyfood.png" LogicalName="Driftwood.Core.emptyfood.png" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
